WEBVTT

1
00:00:06,924 --> 00:00:08,300
[dogs barking]

2
00:00:08,383 --> 00:00:09,510
[birds chirping]

3
00:00:13,472 --> 00:00:14,848
[clattering]

4
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:21,063
- [dogs barking]
- [Craig] Ellie!

5
00:00:27,361 --> 00:00:29,488
They got names,
but I don't know 'em.

6
00:00:29,571 --> 00:00:30,614
[chuckles]

7
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:32,866
My daughter could tell you.

8
00:00:34,868 --> 00:00:36,870
Well, I found out
I became a juror on this trial

9
00:00:36,954 --> 00:00:40,415
when I got a letter in the mail
and opened it.

10
00:00:41,583 --> 00:00:43,293
{\an8}I let my mom look at it and she's, like,

11
00:00:43,377 --> 00:00:46,088
{\an8}"Oh, my God, you're going
to be on the Murdaugh trial."

12
00:00:46,672 --> 00:00:48,257
[dramatic string music plays]

13
00:00:51,969 --> 00:00:54,596
[newscaster 1] <i>We begin with a tragedy</i>
<i>in the Lowcountry overnight.</i>

14
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,723
<i>Two people, a mother and son, are killed.</i>

15
00:00:56,807 --> 00:01:00,018
<i>And now, investigators are looking</i>
<i>for answers about exactly what happened.</i>

16
00:01:00,102 --> 00:01:01,812
[newscaster 2] <i>The victims,</i>
<i>part of the Murdaugh family,</i>

17
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:03,188
<i>a legal and political dynasty.</i>

18
00:01:03,272 --> 00:01:05,065
[newscaster 3]<i> New developments today</i>
<i>in the Alex Murdaugh case.</i>

19
00:01:05,148 --> 00:01:08,193
<i>We just learned authorities</i>
<i>took Alex Murdaugh into custody.</i>

20
00:01:08,277 --> 00:01:12,322
[newscaster 4]<i> Alex Murdaugh, charged with</i>
<i>the double murder of his wife and son.</i>

21
00:01:12,406 --> 00:01:13,657
So much has happened.

22
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:15,200
This family is basically cursed.

23
00:01:15,284 --> 00:01:18,078
[newscaster 5]<i> Murder mystery</i>
<i>that has gripped the nation.</i>

24
00:01:18,161 --> 00:01:19,413
Alex set up a hitman.

25
00:01:19,496 --> 00:01:20,664
We learned some things this morning.

26
00:01:20,747 --> 00:01:22,249
[newscaster 6]
<i>More twists in the now growing legal case.</i>

27
00:01:22,332 --> 00:01:24,626
[newscaster 7]
<i>Suspicion of stealing millions of dollars</i>

28
00:01:24,710 --> 00:01:26,545
<i>of insurance settlement money.</i>

29
00:01:26,628 --> 00:01:29,548
[newscaster 8]
<i>It's just part of this bizarre story.</i>

30
00:01:29,631 --> 00:01:32,467
<i>Prosecutors said</i>
<i>that Murdaugh fatally shot both of them</i>

31
00:01:32,551 --> 00:01:35,554
<i>in order to distract</i>
<i>from his own financial crimes.</i>

32
00:01:35,637 --> 00:01:39,141
<i>This became a fascinating case</i>
<i>and a lot of twists and turns.</i>

33
00:01:39,224 --> 00:01:42,144
<i>What has been dubbed</i>
<i>South Carolina's trial of the century.</i>

34
00:01:42,227 --> 00:01:45,522
Breaking news from South Carolina tonight,
where the verdict is in.

35
00:01:45,606 --> 00:01:47,691
{\an8}Alex Murdaugh is found guilty.

36
00:01:47,774 --> 00:01:49,151
{\an8}- Guilty.
- Guilty.

37
00:01:49,776 --> 00:01:52,029
[Craig] I was convinced
without a reasonable doubt

38
00:01:52,112 --> 00:01:55,657
that he was guilty,
so my verdict was guilty.

39
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:58,452
I thought this was behind us.

40
00:01:58,535 --> 00:01:59,745
{\an8}A stunning development

41
00:01:59,828 --> 00:02:02,831
{\an8}in one of South Carolina's
most high-profile murder cases.

42
00:02:02,914 --> 00:02:06,001
Three years
after the verdict watched by millions,

43
00:02:06,084 --> 00:02:08,211
Alex Murdaugh will get a new trial.

44
00:02:08,295 --> 00:02:10,130
[newscaster 9]
<i>This all centers around the actions</i>

45
00:02:10,213 --> 00:02:12,966
<i>of the county clerk at the time,</i>
<i>a woman named Becky Hill.</i>

46
00:02:13,050 --> 00:02:16,386
[newscaster 10]<i> The justices finding</i>
<i>shocking jury interference</i>

47
00:02:16,470 --> 00:02:18,513
<i>by the court clerk, Becky Hill.</i>

48
00:02:18,597 --> 00:02:22,225
She was a friendly person.
Maybe she was too friendly.

49
00:02:22,309 --> 00:02:25,312
She felt a little bit like the 13th juror.

50
00:02:25,812 --> 00:02:27,397
They got a lot of explaining to do.

51
00:02:27,481 --> 00:02:30,734
And we were all just trying
to get through all the lies.

52
00:02:33,028 --> 00:02:34,738
[dramatic string music ends]

53
00:02:39,034 --> 00:02:40,410
[pensive music plays]

54
00:02:44,581 --> 00:02:46,208
[Bill] This is Walter Street

55
00:02:46,291 --> 00:02:50,629
{\an8}and if you look back this way,
you can see City Hall.

56
00:02:50,712 --> 00:02:53,131
Of course, you see
the palmetto trees here.

57
00:02:53,715 --> 00:02:56,259
Come to South Carolina,
you see a lot of palmettoes.

58
00:02:57,552 --> 00:02:59,513
Probably our two largest employers

59
00:02:59,596 --> 00:03:02,349
{\an8}would be the school district
and the hospital.

60
00:03:02,432 --> 00:03:06,561
We have a lot of agriculture here,
a lot of tourism here.

61
00:03:06,645 --> 00:03:08,438
It's a very welcoming town.

62
00:03:09,439 --> 00:03:11,441
The kind of place
where you would bring people in

63
00:03:11,525 --> 00:03:15,654
and sit on the porch
and have a Coca-Cola,

64
00:03:15,737 --> 00:03:18,573
a glass of iced tea and talk.

65
00:03:19,366 --> 00:03:22,369
All of the things
that make a small town great.

66
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:28,166
[interviewer] Do you think people knew
of Murdaugh before all this?

67
00:03:28,875 --> 00:03:31,753
{\an8}[Bill] Well, the Murdaughs
did not go to school here.

68
00:03:31,837 --> 00:03:33,588
{\an8}They did not grow up here.

69
00:03:33,672 --> 00:03:36,049
{\an8}They were not part of this community.

70
00:03:36,133 --> 00:03:40,679
But because the crime took place
just inside county lines,

71
00:03:41,388 --> 00:03:43,849
we had the trial and all of that, so…

72
00:03:44,766 --> 00:03:48,103
But I think that's like saying,
"Do you know Joe Biden?"

73
00:03:48,186 --> 00:03:51,064
You know, and everybody would say,
"Yes, of course I know Joe Biden,"

74
00:03:51,148 --> 00:03:53,692
but most people don't really know him.

75
00:03:54,234 --> 00:03:56,820
And there'd be the same thing
with the Murdaughs.

76
00:03:58,196 --> 00:03:59,489
[pensive music continues]

77
00:04:04,369 --> 00:04:07,456
[Joe] After nearly a 50-year career
in the law,

78
00:04:07,539 --> 00:04:10,959
you run across some interesting people
and interesting cases.

79
00:04:11,835 --> 00:04:14,629
Had some folks give me a snake.

80
00:04:16,214 --> 00:04:18,508
I don't even remember why that would be.

81
00:04:20,969 --> 00:04:23,805
I think it had something
to do with my personality.

82
00:04:23,889 --> 00:04:27,851
And the ever-present hyena,

83
00:04:27,934 --> 00:04:31,688
which my clients find quite entertaining.

84
00:04:31,772 --> 00:04:34,983
I tell them that that's what happens
to you if you don't pay.

85
00:04:36,902 --> 00:04:38,820
{\an8}South Carolina is a small state.

86
00:04:38,904 --> 00:04:42,991
{\an8}So, we have a correspondingly
small number of lawyers.

87
00:04:43,074 --> 00:04:47,037
And I had occasion to spend time
with Buster Murdaugh,

88
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:50,207
the grandfather of Alex Murdaugh.

89
00:04:51,166 --> 00:04:54,127
He was the solicitor for that circuit.

90
00:04:54,211 --> 00:04:57,339
I knew him fondly
because I was one of the prosecutors.

91
00:04:57,923 --> 00:05:01,009
So, we drank liquor
and we went to conventions.

92
00:05:01,092 --> 00:05:04,054
And once I went into private practice,

93
00:05:04,137 --> 00:05:08,725
I was down there as a defense lawyer,
talking with my old friend, Buster.

94
00:05:10,185 --> 00:05:14,272
{\an8}They had a powerhouse law firm
that's recently been renamed.

95
00:05:14,356 --> 00:05:18,568
It became the predominant law firm
in that part of this state.

96
00:05:18,652 --> 00:05:22,113
So, I've grown up
as a lawyer with their family

97
00:05:22,197 --> 00:05:26,201
and interacting with them,
and I knew Alex, or know Alex.

98
00:05:29,287 --> 00:05:34,125
{\an8}I went down to Colleton County,
thinking that this was a three-week trial.

99
00:05:35,085 --> 00:05:37,462
{\an8}And it lasted six weeks.

100
00:05:37,546 --> 00:05:40,382
And I was there every moment of every day.

101
00:05:40,465 --> 00:05:45,095
So, I contacted the clerk
of court, Becky Hill.

102
00:05:46,721 --> 00:05:50,141
And she made arrangements
to save me a seat.

103
00:05:50,225 --> 00:05:55,772
Becky Hill was the clerk of court,
and their job is to run the courthouse.

104
00:05:55,856 --> 00:05:58,233
But most importantly,
once a trial begins,

105
00:05:59,192 --> 00:06:03,363
to accommodate jurors
with meals and privacy

106
00:06:03,446 --> 00:06:07,909
and to protect them
from any outside interference.

107
00:06:10,078 --> 00:06:11,121
{\an8}[sighs]

108
00:06:12,789 --> 00:06:15,417
{\an8}I don't have a lot to say
about Becky Hill.

109
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:19,170
I think Becky Hill
has said a lot for herself.

110
00:06:19,713 --> 00:06:22,340
{\an8}My name is Rebecca "Becky" Hill

111
00:06:22,424 --> 00:06:25,343
{\an8}and I am a native of Walterboro.

112
00:06:25,427 --> 00:06:28,221
I love my county and I love my job.

113
00:06:28,305 --> 00:06:30,348
Clerk, what do you have to tell?

114
00:06:30,432 --> 00:06:34,644
[Myra] I met her during jury selection.

115
00:06:34,728 --> 00:06:38,023
She was chipper and happy
and asked us to make a list

116
00:06:38,106 --> 00:06:43,069
of drinks or anything, kind of pastries,
that we would want in the mornings,

117
00:06:43,153 --> 00:06:45,530
and thought she was a friendly person.

118
00:06:45,614 --> 00:06:47,490
Maybe she was too friendly.

119
00:06:47,574 --> 00:06:51,453
Always tried to be extra helpful.

120
00:06:52,287 --> 00:06:54,915
[Clifton]<i> Your purpose as jurors</i>

121
00:06:54,998 --> 00:06:59,210
<i>will be to decide the facts of this case.</i>

122
00:07:00,211 --> 00:07:02,631
Well, I thought it was
interesting and crazy

123
00:07:03,465 --> 00:07:07,427
that somebody would actually
murder their wife and their son.

124
00:07:07,510 --> 00:07:12,474
There's always two sides to a case,
so you got to see it through.

125
00:07:13,141 --> 00:07:17,604
I was in that jury room daily,
longer than I would have been at work.

126
00:07:17,687 --> 00:07:21,733
[Clifton] You cannot discuss the case
with family, friends, or anyone else.

127
00:07:21,816 --> 00:07:24,361
[Myra] You could see people
in the audience that you knew.

128
00:07:24,444 --> 00:07:25,820
You see people from news media.

129
00:07:25,904 --> 00:07:29,074
You see politicians,
and you're just like, "Holy shit."

130
00:07:29,157 --> 00:07:31,660
[reporter 1]<i> Did the weapon</i>
<i>used to kill Maggie Murdaugh</i>

131
00:07:31,743 --> 00:07:33,119
<i>belong to her family?</i>

132
00:07:33,203 --> 00:07:36,247
[reporter 2]<i> The gun used to kill Maggie</i>
<i>still has not been recovered,</i>

133
00:07:36,331 --> 00:07:39,125
<i>nor has Paul Murdaugh's rifle.</i>

134
00:07:39,209 --> 00:07:41,544
Seeing all the pictures
and all the evidence,

135
00:07:41,628 --> 00:07:44,255
it's not something
anybody would really want to go through.

136
00:07:44,339 --> 00:07:47,884
[Myra] I can still close my eyes
and see those pictures.

137
00:07:47,968 --> 00:07:48,969
It's horrendous.

138
00:07:49,052 --> 00:07:50,845
[reporter 3]<i> Tonight,</i>
<i>Alex Murdaugh's alibi</i>

139
00:07:50,929 --> 00:07:54,516
<i>potentially destroyed</i>
<i>by his son's own video.</i>

140
00:07:54,599 --> 00:07:57,769
Before the Snapchat video,
I wasn't sure if he did it or not.

141
00:07:57,852 --> 00:08:00,563
[reporter 4]<i> Prosecutors revealing</i>
<i>a 58-second cell phone video,</i>

142
00:08:00,647 --> 00:08:03,108
<i>directly contradicting</i>
<i>Alex Murdaugh's story.</i>

143
00:08:03,191 --> 00:08:05,819
[Craig] That puts him
in the place of the murder.

144
00:08:05,902 --> 00:08:07,487
I pretty much knew it was him.

145
00:08:07,570 --> 00:08:08,655
[indistinct chatter]

146
00:08:08,738 --> 00:08:13,284
If you listen to the video,
they were laughing, they were joking.

147
00:08:13,368 --> 00:08:15,787
It gave me a little bit of pause.

148
00:08:15,870 --> 00:08:17,956
[indistinct chatter over video recording]

149
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:21,751
How is this video proof
that he killed them?

150
00:08:22,502 --> 00:08:24,546
[camera shutters clicking]

151
00:08:25,380 --> 00:08:27,549
[reporter 5]
<i>This coverage is wall-to-wall.</i>

152
00:08:27,632 --> 00:08:30,343
[reporter 6]<i> They haven't seen</i>
<i>this much media attention in the area</i>

153
00:08:30,427 --> 00:08:33,471
<i>since the filming</i>
<i>of </i>Forrest Gump <i>back in 1994.</i>

154
00:08:34,639 --> 00:08:38,143
[Bill] The world came to Walterboro.
We had media here from everywhere.

155
00:08:38,977 --> 00:08:42,022
During the trial, this whole area
was covered with people.

156
00:08:42,105 --> 00:08:46,693
And as the trial progressed,
the line got longer and longer.

157
00:08:46,776 --> 00:08:49,237
And as people
were watching it on television,

158
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:52,449
they started coming here
from all over the place.

159
00:08:52,532 --> 00:08:55,910
And if you can see
this cleared area over there,

160
00:08:55,994 --> 00:08:58,580
a lot of the news media was lined up.

161
00:08:58,663 --> 00:09:03,084
They had tents,
15, 20 satellite trucks here.

162
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:05,253
We had food trucks.

163
00:09:05,336 --> 00:09:06,838
We had porta potties.

164
00:09:06,921 --> 00:09:09,591
We have someone here that rents those,
and they're really nice.

165
00:09:09,674 --> 00:09:11,926
They're called Taj Ma-Stalls.

166
00:09:12,010 --> 00:09:14,804
And so, we had
some Taj Ma-Stalls over there.

167
00:09:14,888 --> 00:09:16,890
- [siren wails]
- [indistinct chatter]

168
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:22,812
Up until we got into the issue
with the clerk of court…

169
00:09:24,355 --> 00:09:27,233
we walked away
feeling really good about what we'd done

170
00:09:27,317 --> 00:09:30,653
and that we had done a good job.
So we were proud.

171
00:09:33,031 --> 00:09:35,784
And what do those two records indicate?

172
00:09:35,867 --> 00:09:38,078
[Myra] During the trial, I was undecided.

173
00:09:38,161 --> 00:09:42,415
I really didn't have
a "He's guilty, he's not guilty."

174
00:09:42,499 --> 00:09:44,167
I had a lot of questions.

175
00:09:44,250 --> 00:09:47,003
And if I'm gonna convict a man of murder,

176
00:09:47,504 --> 00:09:50,256
I need a little bit more
than thoughts and theories.

177
00:09:50,340 --> 00:09:52,926
I mean, there's just a lot of "ifs."

178
00:09:53,009 --> 00:09:54,761
[Clifton] Mr. Murdaugh,
if you'll come forward

179
00:09:54,844 --> 00:10:00,558
and stand in front of me,
and Madam Clerk, if you can swear him in.

180
00:10:00,642 --> 00:10:02,936
[Myra] The day that Alex
was gonna take the stand,

181
00:10:03,019 --> 00:10:06,773
Becky came through the courtroom,
and she was excited.

182
00:10:06,856 --> 00:10:08,900
You could see it in her face.

183
00:10:08,983 --> 00:10:10,985
[Clifton] Have you made a decision
as to whether you're gonna testify?

184
00:10:11,069 --> 00:10:13,947
I am going to testify. I want to testify.

185
00:10:14,030 --> 00:10:17,242
She said, "Oh, my God, y'all,
this is gonna be an epic day."

186
00:10:17,325 --> 00:10:19,452
"Alex is gonna take the stand."

187
00:10:20,036 --> 00:10:22,330
She told us that
we were all gonna be famous.

188
00:10:22,413 --> 00:10:24,833
And she said,
"But I want y'all to listen carefully."

189
00:10:24,916 --> 00:10:30,088
"You need to pay very close attention
to Alex's body movement, his motions."

190
00:10:30,588 --> 00:10:33,508
"And you know, don't be fooled
by what the defense says."

191
00:10:33,591 --> 00:10:35,385
"You're gonna hear a lot of things."

192
00:10:36,302 --> 00:10:38,930
And when she said that,
I just kind of questioned to myself,

193
00:10:39,013 --> 00:10:41,266
"Did she just tell me
that they're gonna lie?"

194
00:10:43,518 --> 00:10:44,561
Good morning.

195
00:10:46,146 --> 00:10:49,440
[Joe] Unheard of.
That is so inappropriate. It's…

196
00:10:50,358 --> 00:10:54,946
I mean, it's even hard for me to conceive
that anybody could think

197
00:10:55,029 --> 00:10:58,324
that that was not gonna be
a problem for them.

198
00:10:58,408 --> 00:10:59,784
[Mr. Griffin] Mr. Murdaugh…

199
00:11:01,161 --> 00:11:02,453
on June 7th…

200
00:11:03,413 --> 00:11:07,292
2021, did you take this gun,
or any gun like it,

201
00:11:07,792 --> 00:11:11,421
and fire it into your wife, Maggie,
causing her death?

202
00:11:13,006 --> 00:11:16,217
Mr. Griffin, I didn't shoot
my wife or my son anytime.

203
00:11:17,218 --> 00:11:18,261
Ever.

204
00:11:20,513 --> 00:11:23,057
[reporter 6]<i> Now, to breaking news</i>
<i>in the Murdaugh murder trial.</i>

205
00:11:23,141 --> 00:11:25,727
[reporter 7]<i> After six weeks</i>
<i>in this Colleton County courtroom,</i>

206
00:11:25,810 --> 00:11:28,438
<i>jurors are weighing the fate</i>
<i>of disbarred attorney, Alex Murdaugh.</i>

207
00:11:28,521 --> 00:11:30,064
[reporter 8] <i>Jury's prepared to work</i>
<i>through the weekend.</i>

208
00:11:30,148 --> 00:11:32,233
[reporter 9] <i>I'd be stunned</i>
<i>if they came back</i>

209
00:11:32,317 --> 00:11:36,196
<i>with a verdict within a day,</i>
<i>because this is a complicated case.</i>

210
00:11:36,279 --> 00:11:37,947
[pensive music continues]

211
00:11:38,907 --> 00:11:40,200
[Myra] The day of deliberation,

212
00:11:40,283 --> 00:11:43,786
I had a phone call conversation
with Becky.

213
00:11:44,454 --> 00:11:48,082
She asked me, "Well, have you made up
your mind about the verdict?"

214
00:11:48,166 --> 00:11:49,125
And I said, "No."

215
00:11:49,209 --> 00:11:51,461
I wanted to hear,
you know, everything to the end

216
00:11:51,544 --> 00:11:53,671
before I, you know, started to deliberate.

217
00:11:54,172 --> 00:11:58,218
And she asked me what made me think
that he was innocent.

218
00:11:58,760 --> 00:12:01,554
And I said, "Well,
there's no murder weapons, you know."

219
00:12:01,638 --> 00:12:03,723
"There's-- Guns are still missing."

220
00:12:03,806 --> 00:12:06,559
And she told me that I needed
to put them out of my head.

221
00:12:06,643 --> 00:12:08,353
They would never be seen again.

222
00:12:08,895 --> 00:12:11,439
I can't honestly say
how I would have voted.

223
00:12:13,942 --> 00:12:16,611
That was the morning I was dismissed.

224
00:12:18,196 --> 00:12:23,284
{\an8}First off this morning,
we have to deal with the issue involving…

225
00:12:25,203 --> 00:12:28,039
{\an8}the removal of a juror.

226
00:12:28,915 --> 00:12:32,543
They said there was an anonymous email
from the public that stated

227
00:12:32,627 --> 00:12:37,507
that I was speaking about the trial
outside of court.

228
00:12:39,634 --> 00:12:41,761
- [interviewer] And were you?
- No.

229
00:12:42,804 --> 00:12:45,181
I spoke to nobody about the trial.

230
00:12:45,265 --> 00:12:48,476
Thank you for your service. I'm not…

231
00:12:49,435 --> 00:12:53,398
suggesting that you intentionally did
anything wrong,

232
00:12:54,023 --> 00:12:58,444
but that in order to preserve
the integrity of the process,

233
00:12:58,528 --> 00:13:01,114
and in fairness
to all the parties involved,

234
00:13:01,197 --> 00:13:06,744
we're going to replace you
with one of the other jurors.

235
00:13:08,705 --> 00:13:11,624
Have you brought everything
that you have?

236
00:13:11,708 --> 00:13:14,836
Have you left some stuff in there?
What do you have in there?

237
00:13:15,336 --> 00:13:17,505
- [Myra] A dozen eggs.
- Say that again.

238
00:13:17,588 --> 00:13:19,632
- [Myra] A dozen eggs.
- "A dozen eggs"?

239
00:13:19,716 --> 00:13:20,800
[Myra] Yeah.

240
00:13:23,219 --> 00:13:28,099
When I was dismissed from court,
I was known as juror 785,

241
00:13:28,182 --> 00:13:31,602
or more publicly as the "Egg Juror."

242
00:13:33,146 --> 00:13:35,189
[Clifton]
Three-five-four will become part of the…

243
00:13:35,273 --> 00:13:40,069
That was an extraordinary thing to happen,
to excuse a juror…

244
00:13:41,279 --> 00:13:44,657
an hour before the case
would otherwise have been handed

245
00:13:44,741 --> 00:13:47,827
to she and the other 11 jurors
that had sat there

246
00:13:47,910 --> 00:13:49,996
listening to the entire trial.

247
00:13:51,039 --> 00:13:54,292
So, everyone wondered
what in the world was going on.

248
00:13:56,294 --> 00:14:00,506
[Craig] We walked into deliberations
and everybody was excited.

249
00:14:00,590 --> 00:14:06,512
You know, we took an initial vote
and nine of us said guilty.

250
00:14:07,096 --> 00:14:11,934
Two of us said not guilty,
and one of us were undecided.

251
00:14:13,227 --> 00:14:15,355
So, we gotta deliberate some more.

252
00:14:16,522 --> 00:14:18,274
We had to look at more evidence.

253
00:14:18,816 --> 00:14:22,945
We looked at the guns.
We looked at the Snapchat video.

254
00:14:23,029 --> 00:14:25,365
- [woman 1] Come here, Bubba!
- [woman 2] Catch, quick.

255
00:14:25,448 --> 00:14:27,116
[Craig] Everybody was shocked.

256
00:14:27,825 --> 00:14:31,496
And I think they really could
hear it better in the jury room,

257
00:14:31,579 --> 00:14:34,749
so they could actually pay attention
a little better.

258
00:14:34,832 --> 00:14:37,210
And I think the Snapchat video swayed…

259
00:14:38,002 --> 00:14:39,962
their vote.

260
00:14:40,671 --> 00:14:44,675
In the term of 2022, July,

261
00:14:44,759 --> 00:14:48,262
the state versus
Richard Alexander Murdaugh, defendant,

262
00:14:48,346 --> 00:14:51,933
indictment for murder, guilty verdict,

263
00:14:52,016 --> 00:14:56,104
signed by the forelady, 3-2-23.

264
00:14:56,187 --> 00:14:58,147
[reporter 10]
How could you kill your wife and son?

265
00:14:58,231 --> 00:14:59,857
[reporter 11]
What do you think of the verdict, Alex?

266
00:14:59,941 --> 00:15:02,318
- [reporter 12] Why did you lie?
- [newscaster 11]<i> There is breaking news.</i>

267
00:15:02,402 --> 00:15:06,280
<i>Alex Murdaugh is found guilty</i>
<i>of murdering his wife and 22-year-old son.</i>

268
00:15:06,364 --> 00:15:07,865
[newscaster 12]
<i>Good evening, we begin with breaking news.</i>

269
00:15:07,949 --> 00:15:09,450
<i>After just about three hours</i>
<i>of deliberation…</i>

270
00:15:09,534 --> 00:15:10,618
[newscaster 13]<i> Three hours.</i>

271
00:15:10,701 --> 00:15:14,163
<i>Three hours to find disgraced lawyer,</i>
<i>Alex Murdaugh, guilty.</i>

272
00:15:14,247 --> 00:15:18,793
Today's verdict proves that no one,
no matter who you are in society,

273
00:15:18,876 --> 00:15:20,128
is above the law.

274
00:15:22,213 --> 00:15:23,131
Thank you.

275
00:15:23,214 --> 00:15:26,050
[Joe] Shortly after the jury verdict,

276
00:15:26,134 --> 00:15:31,347
public interest went from,
you know, 70 to 200.

277
00:15:31,431 --> 00:15:33,433
There are so many loose ends
with this one.

278
00:15:33,516 --> 00:15:36,519
{\an8}I still actually think that
there was not enough to convict him.

279
00:15:36,602 --> 00:15:38,396
{\an8}I hope he rots in jail.

280
00:15:38,479 --> 00:15:40,398
{\an8}Anyone else feel like your entire life

281
00:15:40,481 --> 00:15:42,400
{\an8}is revolved around
the Murdaugh trial right now?

282
00:15:42,483 --> 00:15:45,736
After trial, I got contacted
by a whole slew of people.

283
00:15:45,820 --> 00:15:47,405
I got in with Dr. Phil.

284
00:15:47,947 --> 00:15:50,575
{\an8}We've been analyzing
the body language from Alex Murdaugh.

285
00:15:50,658 --> 00:15:52,076
{\an8}[Craig] And <i>Good Morning America.</i>

286
00:15:52,160 --> 00:15:54,579
{\an8}[newscaster 14]
<i>Juror Craig Moyer breaking his silence.</i>

287
00:15:54,662 --> 00:15:58,249
{\an8}Joining us now are jurors James McDowell,
Gwen Generette, and Amie Williams.

288
00:15:58,332 --> 00:15:59,584
{\an8}Good morning to all of you.

289
00:15:59,667 --> 00:16:03,087
{\an8}By the way, guess who's spoken out?
OJ Simpson even weighed in.

290
00:16:03,171 --> 00:16:06,841
I went to the Murdaugh auction,
and this is what I bought.

291
00:16:06,924 --> 00:16:09,719
One lady paid so much money for a vacuum

292
00:16:09,802 --> 00:16:12,138
that was, like, still full of dirt
from their home.

293
00:16:12,221 --> 00:16:13,097
Oh, my gosh.

294
00:16:13,181 --> 00:16:15,308
I mean, it just went, "Boom."

295
00:16:20,104 --> 00:16:21,689
[pensive music ends]

296
00:16:22,857 --> 00:16:25,526
[Myra] After the trial,
it kind of blew up with…

297
00:16:26,652 --> 00:16:29,447
what happened,
and everybody had, you know…

298
00:16:29,530 --> 00:16:34,494
they wanted to do a story,
and I just didn't want any part of it.

299
00:16:34,577 --> 00:16:36,120
[pensive music plays]

300
00:16:38,164 --> 00:16:43,211
[Myra] It got me to a point
that I had to go see the counselor

301
00:16:43,294 --> 00:16:46,047
who helped me to deal with

302
00:16:46,130 --> 00:16:50,551
some of the social media posts
that had been put out about me.

303
00:16:51,469 --> 00:16:56,307
Stupid comments that I don't even know
where they would come up with this stuff.

304
00:16:56,390 --> 00:16:58,601
But it was kind of hilarious.

305
00:16:58,684 --> 00:17:02,063
I read so much about myself
I didn't even know who I was.

306
00:17:04,398 --> 00:17:06,901
She wanted to reclaim her life
and her privacy.

307
00:17:06,984 --> 00:17:09,737
{\an8}So, my first responsibility for her

308
00:17:09,820 --> 00:17:12,615
{\an8}and ultimately
for the second juror, Juror Z…

309
00:17:13,407 --> 00:17:17,662
was to get the press off of their back.
And then, as we talked,

310
00:17:17,745 --> 00:17:23,459
I learned more about
what had gone on backstage.

311
00:17:23,543 --> 00:17:26,629
And it caused me great concern,
and I knew…

312
00:17:27,588 --> 00:17:28,881
uh, that…

313
00:17:29,757 --> 00:17:34,720
the defense lawyers
were talking about interviewing jurors.

314
00:17:34,804 --> 00:17:38,975
{\an8}Attorneys for Alex Murdaugh
are demanding a new trial

315
00:17:39,058 --> 00:17:41,394
{\an8}and a federal investigation,
citing allegations

316
00:17:41,477 --> 00:17:44,230
{\an8}that a court clerk
engaged in jury tampering.

317
00:17:44,313 --> 00:17:47,942
They then filed a motion for a new trial
based upon jury tampering.

318
00:17:48,568 --> 00:17:52,238
And that led to an extraordinary hearing.

319
00:17:54,323 --> 00:17:55,533
[Jean] Please be seated.

320
00:17:57,743 --> 00:18:01,414
{\an8}Juror Z, did you hear Ms. Becky Hill

321
00:18:01,497 --> 00:18:05,084
{\an8}make any comment about this case
before your verdict?

322
00:18:05,751 --> 00:18:08,504
- [Juror Z] Yes, ma'am.
- What did Ms. Hill say?

323
00:18:11,215 --> 00:18:12,800
{\an8}[Juror Z] To watch his actions.

324
00:18:13,426 --> 00:18:14,969
{\an8}[Jean] To watch his actions.

325
00:18:15,678 --> 00:18:16,721
{\an8}What else?

326
00:18:18,431 --> 00:18:21,726
{\an8}- [Juror Z] To watch him closely.
- [Jean] To watch him closely.

327
00:18:21,809 --> 00:18:23,311
Was your verdict…

328
00:18:24,854 --> 00:18:28,608
influenced in any way
by the communications

329
00:18:28,691 --> 00:18:30,943
of the clerk of court in this case?

330
00:18:31,027 --> 00:18:32,111
[Juror Z] Yes, ma'am.

331
00:18:32,194 --> 00:18:34,614
She made it seem like
he was already guilty.

332
00:18:37,158 --> 00:18:39,744
Thank you, Your Honor.
The state calls Rebecca Hill.

333
00:18:40,494 --> 00:18:41,412
[clears throat]

334
00:18:44,749 --> 00:18:45,875
[lawyer] Did you…

335
00:18:46,626 --> 00:18:51,339
interact with any juror
in an attempt to influence their view

336
00:18:51,422 --> 00:18:54,008
of the facts in the State v Murdaugh case?

337
00:18:54,091 --> 00:18:55,343
No.

338
00:18:55,426 --> 00:18:58,888
At any time, did you tell the jury
"not to be fooled"

339
00:18:58,971 --> 00:19:01,974
by evidence presented
by Mr. Murdaugh's attorneys?

340
00:19:02,058 --> 00:19:03,225
I did not.

341
00:19:03,309 --> 00:19:06,103
At any time did you instruct the jury

342
00:19:06,187 --> 00:19:08,689
to watch him closely
and look at his actions?

343
00:19:08,773 --> 00:19:10,274
I did not.

344
00:19:10,358 --> 00:19:14,945
At any time did you instruct the jury
or tell the jury to look at his movements?

345
00:19:15,029 --> 00:19:16,030
No.

346
00:19:16,113 --> 00:19:19,492
Anything along those lines
that was meant to influence

347
00:19:19,575 --> 00:19:22,328
- the jurors against Mr. Murdaugh?
- No.

348
00:19:23,829 --> 00:19:25,498
[dramatic music plays]

349
00:19:33,589 --> 00:19:37,218
[Myra] I wish she would come out
and tell the truth and say why.

350
00:19:37,301 --> 00:19:40,346
I wish more than anything
that she would tell the truth.

351
00:19:40,429 --> 00:19:45,017
Among the reasons that have
been posited out there are the--

352
00:19:45,726 --> 00:19:51,065
She did it all because she has
some problem with the Murdaughs.

353
00:19:51,148 --> 00:19:55,027
Another theory is that this was all
about the book she was writing,

354
00:19:55,111 --> 00:20:00,324
that she figured the only ending
that would sell books is a conviction.

355
00:20:00,408 --> 00:20:01,575
[tense music plays]

356
00:20:07,957 --> 00:20:10,209
[Neil] So, this is trialwatchers.com,

357
00:20:10,292 --> 00:20:14,755
and we try to celebrate true crime fans
from all over the country.

358
00:20:15,381 --> 00:20:18,926
And we decided to focus
on the Murdaugh trial.

359
00:20:19,719 --> 00:20:22,596
[Melissa] I first heard the name Becky

360
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:25,683
{\an8}when I was standing
in line at the courthouse.

361
00:20:26,308 --> 00:20:28,269
Well, she said, I want to write a book.

362
00:20:28,352 --> 00:20:32,815
And I said, "I should connect you
with my husband. He's a former newscaster.

363
00:20:32,898 --> 00:20:36,527
{\an8}She had already had the title
of the book in mind.

364
00:20:36,610 --> 00:20:38,904
{\an8}She already knew
what she wanted the focus to be,

365
00:20:38,988 --> 00:20:42,241
which is to take somebody,
kind of, behind the curtain

366
00:20:42,324 --> 00:20:45,327
during what was known
as the trial of the century.

367
00:20:45,411 --> 00:20:49,623
They wanted autographs from us.
They wanted autographs from the bailiffs.

368
00:20:49,707 --> 00:20:53,127
Becky and I probably communicated
every other day.

369
00:20:53,210 --> 00:20:54,503
We would do a Zoom call.

370
00:20:54,587 --> 00:20:59,175
I'd be laying back with my neck pillow
like I'm on an airplane or something,

371
00:20:59,258 --> 00:21:02,136
and I would ask her
a number of different questions,

372
00:21:02,219 --> 00:21:06,599
gather all the information, and then
I would basically do a rough draft.

373
00:21:06,682 --> 00:21:10,311
Like, do they recognize you
as the person that read the verdict?

374
00:21:10,853 --> 00:21:11,729
Oh, yeah.

375
00:21:11,812 --> 00:21:15,733
They'll say, "You're the one
who read the verdict." Yeah. Yeah.

376
00:21:15,816 --> 00:21:17,735
[both laughing]

377
00:21:17,818 --> 00:21:22,031
So we ended up publishing it
in a span of about four months.

378
00:21:23,532 --> 00:21:28,120
Becky Hill had a front row seat
every single day during that trial.

379
00:21:28,204 --> 00:21:31,207
{\an8}<i>There will be a book tour</i>
<i>that I'm going to be on,</i>

380
00:21:31,290 --> 00:21:36,295
{\an8}<i>and that's coming up in the next 14 days,</i>
<i>so stay tuned for where I'll be.</i>

381
00:21:36,378 --> 00:21:40,841
A book event got set up,
and there was a line around the building.

382
00:21:40,925 --> 00:21:44,678
And every morning, Alex would walk in
and say, "Good morning, Miss Becky."

383
00:21:45,179 --> 00:21:48,724
[Neil] We sold 250 books
within three hours

384
00:21:48,808 --> 00:21:50,935
and she autographed every single one

385
00:21:51,018 --> 00:21:56,524
and talked to people.
She was one of them. That's her people.

386
00:21:57,399 --> 00:22:01,237
That's why we feel like she just
really naturally got caught up.

387
00:22:02,238 --> 00:22:05,449
There were things along the way,
looking back…

388
00:22:06,492 --> 00:22:09,578
um, that maybe…

389
00:22:11,121 --> 00:22:15,292
were oversharing sometimes
and it gave me a pause.

390
00:22:15,918 --> 00:22:20,256
Everybody that we've encountered
is 100 percent that he's guilty.

391
00:22:20,339 --> 00:22:21,340
Really?

392
00:22:21,423 --> 00:22:24,718
So we haven't met anybody
who thinks he didn't do it.

393
00:22:27,888 --> 00:22:31,392
[Neil] At the crime scene,
when all the jurors and Becky

394
00:22:31,475 --> 00:22:35,563
and all the attorneys went out there
and actually walked around

395
00:22:35,646 --> 00:22:38,148
and she explained how eerie it was.

396
00:22:38,732 --> 00:22:40,734
[Becky] There were several
of us women all together,

397
00:22:40,818 --> 00:22:44,446
and we walked over
to where Maggie would have been laying.

398
00:22:45,322 --> 00:22:48,534
We all looked at one another
and spoke very softly.

399
00:22:49,034 --> 00:22:52,538
This is where she was.
This was her final moments.

400
00:22:55,875 --> 00:23:01,589
What must she have been thinking
to see her son with his head blown off,

401
00:23:01,672 --> 00:23:04,633
because she had to have seen that
before it turned on her,

402
00:23:05,259 --> 00:23:07,386
and then knowing who had done that.

403
00:23:08,554 --> 00:23:12,725
Becky wrote, "As a clerk of court,
we're supposed to be Switzerland."

404
00:23:13,350 --> 00:23:17,479
But as we were riding back
on the way to the courthouse,

405
00:23:17,563 --> 00:23:20,232
when there were a few court officials
in the vehicle,

406
00:23:20,774 --> 00:23:24,778
they actually discussed
what they witnessed.

407
00:23:24,862 --> 00:23:31,201
"And as a regular person," she said,
"we all felt Alex was guilty."

408
00:23:31,285 --> 00:23:35,706
- [Melissa] And that's what she told us.
- Yeah. And that's in the book. Yeah.

409
00:23:35,789 --> 00:23:39,043
She was the clerk of court,
but I almost got the impression

410
00:23:39,126 --> 00:23:42,755
that she felt a little bit
like the 13th juror

411
00:23:42,838 --> 00:23:45,341
and that she was listening
to the testimony

412
00:23:45,424 --> 00:23:49,470
and she was kind of making up her own mind
as she went along.

413
00:23:49,553 --> 00:23:51,180
[birds chirping]

414
00:23:52,765 --> 00:23:54,600
{\an8}[indistinct chatter]

415
00:23:58,938 --> 00:24:00,856
[pensive music plays]

416
00:24:03,609 --> 00:24:06,111
- [photographer] Bill.
- [Bill] How you doing, buddy?

417
00:24:06,820 --> 00:24:07,905
Okay.

418
00:24:08,447 --> 00:24:13,702
{\an8}Today, we filed a lawsuit on behalf
of Richard Alexander Murdaugh Senior

419
00:24:14,203 --> 00:24:16,205
{\an8}versus Rebecca Hill.

420
00:24:16,288 --> 00:24:19,208
With the South Carolina
Supreme Court's ruling,

421
00:24:19,291 --> 00:24:22,002
it has been adjudged
that she deprived him

422
00:24:22,086 --> 00:24:23,963
of a right to a fair trial.

423
00:24:24,046 --> 00:24:26,548
We have a lot of questions
that we would like answered.

424
00:24:27,466 --> 00:24:29,093
Was she a lone wolf?

425
00:24:29,927 --> 00:24:36,225
I'll tell you this removal
of the egg lady juror is very suspicious.

426
00:24:36,308 --> 00:24:39,645
The way she was targeted, it just…

427
00:24:42,064 --> 00:24:43,399
it's very curious.

428
00:24:44,733 --> 00:24:47,152
And we hope
to get to the bottom of that.

429
00:24:48,112 --> 00:24:50,614
[Alan] The defense's job
is to muddy the waters,

430
00:24:51,240 --> 00:24:53,200
to throw out
a thousand conspiracy theories

431
00:24:53,283 --> 00:24:55,744
and get people thinking
about everything else

432
00:24:55,828 --> 00:24:57,913
except about the facts of the case.

433
00:24:59,331 --> 00:25:02,876
{\an8}I was involved in the Murdaugh trial
as the state Attorney General.

434
00:25:03,585 --> 00:25:06,088
I believe we did it right the first time.

435
00:25:06,171 --> 00:25:08,173
It was not our fault
this thing was reversed.

436
00:25:08,257 --> 00:25:09,717
The Supreme Court themselves says

437
00:25:09,800 --> 00:25:12,428
this falls solely
on the shoulders of one person.

438
00:25:12,511 --> 00:25:15,347
That was the Colleton County
Clerk of Court, Becky Hill.

439
00:25:15,431 --> 00:25:17,182
Obviously none of us knew about it.

440
00:25:17,266 --> 00:25:21,520
When we became aware, I sent that case
to an independent prosecutor

441
00:25:21,603 --> 00:25:25,441
who came to the conclusion
that Ms. Hill's conduct was inappropriate,

442
00:25:25,524 --> 00:25:28,485
but it was harmless error
as it related to the conviction.

443
00:25:28,569 --> 00:25:30,362
But the Supreme Court
went in another direction,

444
00:25:30,446 --> 00:25:32,448
and we're going to act accordingly.

445
00:25:33,198 --> 00:25:35,826
[Richard] Over the weekend,
we learned that the Attorney General

446
00:25:35,909 --> 00:25:38,996
{\an8}has announced he's considering
the death penalty in this case.

447
00:25:39,997 --> 00:25:42,041
He's probably talking
to his political consultants

448
00:25:42,124 --> 00:25:45,461
who thought that was a good soundbite
for his governor's campaign.

449
00:25:45,544 --> 00:25:49,006
And then we've seen this process
since the beginning of this case

450
00:25:49,089 --> 00:25:54,720
where Alan Wilson plays politics
as opposed to playing prosecutor.

451
00:25:55,345 --> 00:25:57,598
They got a lot of explaining to do.

452
00:25:57,681 --> 00:26:01,351
Yes, I am a candidate for governor,
but my role as the attorney general

453
00:26:01,435 --> 00:26:04,313
is to set the stage for the next trial.

454
00:26:04,396 --> 00:26:06,982
The Supreme Court
reversing a case like this

455
00:26:07,066 --> 00:26:09,610
is obviously going to affect
everyone's trial strategies

456
00:26:09,693 --> 00:26:11,987
because it's a movie
we've all seen before.

457
00:26:12,071 --> 00:26:14,406
It's like the jump scares,
you know when they're coming.

458
00:26:14,490 --> 00:26:17,910
And if we're starting over
at ground zero, um,

459
00:26:17,993 --> 00:26:20,662
then we're going to consider
and evaluate everything.

460
00:26:20,746 --> 00:26:23,040
People believe this
that if you have money and power,

461
00:26:23,123 --> 00:26:24,124
you have one justice system.

462
00:26:24,208 --> 00:26:27,169
If you don't have money and power,
you have a different justice system.

463
00:26:27,252 --> 00:26:29,671
People said
there's no way someone like that

464
00:26:29,755 --> 00:26:31,882
will be convicted in South Carolina.

465
00:26:31,965 --> 00:26:33,092
We did that the first trial,

466
00:26:33,175 --> 00:26:35,094
and I believe we'll do that
on the second trial.

467
00:26:35,177 --> 00:26:36,762
[pensive music ends]

468
00:26:37,888 --> 00:26:39,681
[ominous music plays]

469
00:27:13,590 --> 00:27:17,302
[Bill] One thing I was thinking about
is how many victims there are

470
00:27:17,386 --> 00:27:18,512
in all of this.

471
00:27:18,595 --> 00:27:21,390
It's not just Alex's family.

472
00:27:21,473 --> 00:27:24,476
There were so many
other people who were impacted.

473
00:27:35,154 --> 00:27:39,658
[Myra] My husband got worried about me
because I was starting to break down.

474
00:27:39,741 --> 00:27:45,289
And I could talk to him,
but it wasn't the same

475
00:27:45,372 --> 00:27:49,835
as getting somebody
that actually could break it down

476
00:27:49,918 --> 00:27:54,006
and explain it to me how to get past it.

477
00:27:59,678 --> 00:28:02,014
- [interviewer] Do you need a moment?
- I'm good.

478
00:28:04,349 --> 00:28:05,434
It was a lot.

479
00:28:06,852 --> 00:28:08,478
[ominous music continues]

480
00:28:14,818 --> 00:28:18,238
I feel very relieved that I have nothing
to do with the second trial.

481
00:28:19,281 --> 00:28:20,782
[ominous music ends]

482
00:28:20,866 --> 00:28:23,619
I don't know. I think they're blowing it
all out of proportion

483
00:28:23,702 --> 00:28:24,953
is what they're doing.

484
00:28:28,373 --> 00:28:30,834
I don't remember anybody
talking about tampering

485
00:28:30,918 --> 00:28:34,087
or influencing anybody or anything.

486
00:28:37,090 --> 00:28:40,219
There was times
I wish I never was involved in this.

487
00:28:40,761 --> 00:28:43,764
I'm like, "Oh, Lord,
they're still talking about it."

488
00:28:43,847 --> 00:28:45,015
[newscaster 15] <i>The saga continues…</i>

489
00:28:45,098 --> 00:28:47,142
[newscaster 16] <i>A second chance to face…</i>

490
00:28:47,226 --> 00:28:48,644
[newscaster 17]
<i>But they'll get a brand-new jury.</i>

491
00:28:48,727 --> 00:28:49,937
<i>Now, this will not be the same jury…</i>

492
00:28:50,020 --> 00:28:52,689
[newscaster 18]<i> Will disgraced attorney</i>
<i>Alex Murdaugh be acquitted</i>

493
00:28:52,773 --> 00:28:55,817
<i>of murdering his wife</i>
<i>and son in a retrial?</i>

494
00:28:57,277 --> 00:29:01,406
I would blame, I guess, Murdaugh.
I mean…

495
00:29:02,407 --> 00:29:04,493
Nobody else to blame except for him.

496
00:29:06,662 --> 00:29:12,042
Especially if he's guilty,
which I sincerely believe he is,

497
00:29:12,584 --> 00:29:15,254
but we'll see.

498
00:29:17,673 --> 00:29:18,924
{\an8}[rifle fires]

499
00:29:20,217 --> 00:29:22,094
[upbeat music plays]

500
00:30:18,608 --> 00:30:20,652
[upbeat music ends]